\title{When Does a Rounded Record Certify an Exact Value?\\[0.4em]
\large Certifiability, closed-form laws and invariance for the cyclic
splitting-type channel}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A reproducibility audit compares a recorded headline number with the output of a
fresh re-run of the same pipeline and declares the two ``identical to four
decimals''. We analyse the mathematical content of that practice for a concrete
family of arithmetic information quantities and show that it splits into two
disjoint regimes governed by the arithmetic of the recorded value itself.

Let $\Z/n$ carry the \emph{splitting type} $T(a)=n/\gcd(n,a)$, the order of a
residue, and record two derived quantities: the type entropy $\HT(n)$, the
Shannon entropy in bits of $T$ under the uniform distribution, and the pair
capacity $\Ipair(n)=I(K;N)$, the mutual information between the unordered type
pair $K=\{T(x),T(y)\}$ of a uniform pair and its norm class $N=x+y \bmod n$.

We prove: (i) a \emph{separation theorem} --- distinct rationals with
denominators $q,r$ differ by at least $1/qr$ --- whence four-decimal agreement
\emph{proves} bit-for-bit equality whenever both denominators are at most $70$,
a threshold that fails since $|1/100-1/101|=1/10100<10^{-4}$; (ii) a
\emph{two-adic entropy law} $\HT(2^k)=2-2^{1-k}$, strictly increasing with a hard
two-bit ceiling and inter-row gap $\HT(2^k)-\HT(2^j)\ge 2^{-j}$ for $j<k$, which
certifies rows for all $k\le 13$ independently of denominator size; (iii) an
\emph{irrationality obstruction} --- $\Ipair(6)=\log_2 3-1/9$ is irrational, so
no finite decimal record can equal it, while the enclosure
$1.58496<\log_2 3<1.58497$, certified by the integer inequalities
$2^{1054}<3^{665}$ and $3^{306}<2^{485}$, validates the stored value to the
claimed precision; (iv) a \emph{rationality dichotomy}: on constructible orders
--- those $n$ all of whose divisors have power-of-two totient, equivalently the
Gauss--Wantzel constructible $n$ --- $\HT(n)$ is rational precisely when $n$ is a
power of two; and (v) an \emph{invariance characterisation}: the record is
unchanged under every change of generator of the cyclic group, and a readout is
so invariant if and only if it factors through the splitting type, which is
therefore the universal reproducible observable of the channel.

The conclusion is a reframing of the audit practice: four-decimal agreement is a
theorem about bounded denominators, not a statement about pipelines, and a
record is reproducible by construction exactly when it is invariant under the
arbitrary choices in its own definition.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} reproducibility; Farey dissection; splitting type;
Shannon entropy; mutual information; Jacobsthal numbers; irrationality;
constructible polygons; Galois invariance.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The practice and the question}

Computational mathematics stores numbers. A pipeline is run, a value such as
$1.3125$ or $1.4738$ is written into a table, and the table is cited. Later the
pipeline is run again --- same fixed seeds, same inputs --- the two printed
values are compared digit by digit, and if they agree to the recorded precision
the entry is declared \emph{reproduced}.

The practice is sound as an operational control: it detects code rot, changed
defaults, uncontrolled randomness and platform drift. But taken literally the
verdict is a statement about two decimal strings, and it is worth asking what
mathematical statement, if any, it establishes about the two \emph{numbers}.

Three questions organise this paper.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{(Q1) Certifiability.} Under what hypotheses does agreement of two
  values to $k$ decimals force them to be equal?
  \item \textbf{(Q2) Stratification.} For a concrete family of recorded
  quantities, which entries satisfy those hypotheses and which provably cannot?
  \item \textbf{(Q3) Construction.} Is there a notion of reproducibility that
  does not depend on re-running anything --- one where the record is forced by
  the definition of the observable rather than by the determinism of the code?
\end{itemize}
The answers are, respectively: a Farey-type separation bound; a dichotomy indexed
by classical constructibility of the regular $n$-gon; and a universality theorem
identifying the unique observable whose record survives every arbitrary
coordinate choice.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Separation and the four-decimal certificate (\S\ref{sec:sep}).
  \item Closed-form laws for the two-adic tower, with monotonicity, a two-bit
  ceiling and gap certification (\S\ref{sec:tower}).
  \item The irrational stratum and an explicit enclosure (\S\ref{sec:irr}).
  \item A rationality dichotomy on constructible orders (\S\ref{sec:dich}).
  \item Invariance under change of generator and its converse
  (\S\ref{sec:inv}).
  \item Algorithms, numerics and a taxonomy of audit verdicts
  (\S\ref{sec:algo}--\S\ref{sec:disc}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The cyclic splitting-type channel}

Throughout $n\ge 1$ is an integer, $\Z/n=\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, all entropies are in
bits, and $\varphi$ is Euler's totient.

\begin{definition}[Splitting type]
For $a\in\Z/n$ set $T(a)=n/\gcd(n,a)$, the order of $a$ in the additive group
$\Z/n$. Arithmetically, if $\Z/n\cong\operatorname{Gal}(L/\Q)$ for a cyclic
extension $L$ and $a$ is the Frobenius class of an unramified prime $p$, then
$T(a)$ is the residue degree of $p$, so $p$ splits into $n/T(a)$ primes of degree
$T(a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Type entropy]
Draw $a$ uniformly from $\Z/n$. Since $\#\{a: T(a)=d\}=\varphi(d)$ for each
$d\mid n$, the law of $T$ is $\Pr[T=d]=\varphi(d)/n$ and
\[
  \HT(n)=-\sum_{d\mid n}\frac{\varphi(d)}{n}\log_2\frac{\varphi(d)}{n}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Divisor formula]\label{lem:div}
For $n\ge1$,
\[
  \HT(n)=\log_2 n-\frac1n\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(d)\log_2\varphi(d).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand the logarithm of the quotient and use $\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(d)=n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Semiprime pair channel]
Draw $(x,y)$ uniformly from $\Z/n\times\Z/n$ and record the unordered type pair
$K=\{T(x),T(y)\}$ and the norm class $N=x+y \bmod n$, the group element attached
to the product. The \emph{pair capacity} is
\[
  \Ipair(n)=I(K;N)=H(K)+H(N)-H(K,N)=H(K)-\frac1n\sum_{c\in\Z/n}H(K\mid N=c),
\]
the number of bits about the joint splitting behaviour of two hidden factors that
leak through observation of their product.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Recorded values]
Direct enumeration gives $\HT(2)=1$, $\HT(4)=3/2$, $\HT(8)=7/4$, $\HT(16)=15/8$
and $\Ipair(4)=5/4$, $\Ipair(8)=21/16$, $\Ipair(16)=85/64$,
$\Ipair(6)=\log_2 3-1/9$. Rounded to four decimals these are the stored records
$1.0000$, $1.5000$, $1.7500$, $1.8750$ and $1.2500$, $1.3125$, $1.3281$,
$1.4738$.
\end{example}

\section{Certifiability: the separation theorem}\label{sec:sep}

\begin{theorem}[Separation of bounded-denominator rationals]\label{thm:sep}
Let $a,b\in\Z$ and $q,r\in\N_{>0}$. If $\left|\frac aq-\frac br\right|<\frac1{qr}$
then $\frac aq=\frac br$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(a/q-b/r)qr=ar-bq$ is an integer of absolute value $<1$, hence $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The bound is attained]
For every $Q\ge1$ the distinct rationals $1/Q$ and $0/1$ satisfy
$|1/Q-0/1|=1/(Q\cdot1)$; the strict inequality in Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} cannot be
relaxed to $\le$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Precision certificate at level $Q$]\label{cor:Q}
If $0<q,r\le Q$ and $|a/q-b/r|<1/Q^2$ then $a/q=b/r$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$qr\le Q^2$, so $1/Q^2\le1/(qr)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}.
\end{proof}

The quantity $1/Q^2$ is the \emph{Farey resolution} at level $Q$: fractions of
denominator at most $Q$ form a discrete scaffold with gaps $1/(qr)\ge 1/Q^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Four-decimal certificate]\label{thm:four}
If $a/q$ and $b/r$ have $0<q,r\le 70$ and $|a/q-b/r|<10^{-4}$ then $a/q=b/r$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$70^2=4900\le10^4$, so $10^{-4}\le1/70^2$; apply Corollary~\ref{cor:Q}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
$\left|\frac1{100}-\frac1{101}\right|=\frac1{10100}<10^{-4}$ while
$\frac1{100}\ne\frac1{101}$. No four-decimal certificate can hold for
denominators up to $101$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The largest denominator bound surviving at precision $10^{-4}$ is $Q=99$, since
$1/99^2=1.0203\ldots\times10^{-4}>10^{-4}\ge1/100^2$. The value $70$ is the
conservative working bound: comfortably above every denominator occurring in the
record and comfortably below the failure point.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Certified keystones]\label{cor:keystones}
Suppose a re-run produces a rational $b/r$ with $0<r\le70$. If
$|b/r-\Ipair(4)|<10^{-4}$ then $b/r=\Ipair(4)=5/4$; if
$|b/r-\Ipair(8)|<10^{-4}$ then $b/r=\Ipair(8)=21/16$; if
$|b/r-\Ipair(16)|<10^{-4}$ then $b/r=\Ipair(16)=85/64$; if
$|b/r-\HT(4)|<10^{-4}$ then $b/r=\HT(4)=3/2$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The recorded values have denominators $4$, $16$, $64$, $2$, all at most $70$;
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:four}.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:keystones} assumes nothing about algorithm, precision, seeds
or machine: in this stratum reproduction is certified before the re-run happens,
and the re-run merely supplies the inequality.

\section{From four rows to a law: the two-adic tower}\label{sec:tower}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sum}
$\sum_{i<k}i\,2^i=(k-2)2^k+2$ for all $k\ge0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction; the step is $(k-2)2^k+2+k2^k=(k-1)2^{k+1}+2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:phisum}
$\sum_{d\mid 2^k}\varphi(d)\log_2\varphi(d)=(k-2)2^k+2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The divisors are $2^0,\dots,2^k$; the term $d=1$ vanishes and
$\varphi(2^{i+1})=2^i$ contributes $i2^i$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:sum}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Two-adic entropy law]\label{thm:law}
For every $k\ge0$, $\HT(2^k)=2-2/2^k=2-2^{1-k}$; for $k\ge1$ this is the fraction
$(2^k-1)/2^{k-1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:div} with $n=2^k$ gives $\HT(2^k)=k-((k-2)2^k+2)/2^k
=k-(k-2)-2^{1-k}=2-2^{1-k}$, using Lemma~\ref{lem:phisum}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Audit consistency]
The four recorded rows $\HT(2)=1$, $\HT(4)=3/2$, $\HT(8)=7/4$, $\HT(16)=15/8$ are
exactly the instances $k=1,2,3,4$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:law}; the consistency check
is upgraded from a coincidence of digits to a specialisation of a theorem.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity and the two-bit ceiling]
$\HT(2^k)<\HT(2^{k+1})$ and $\HT(2^k)<2$ for every $k$, and $\HT(2^k)\to2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The subtracted term $2^{1-k}$ is positive, halves at each step and tends to $0$.
\end{proof}

However large the cyclic $2$-group, the splitting type of a uniform residue
carries strictly fewer than two bits, and exactly two in the limit.

\begin{theorem}[Row gap]\label{thm:gap}
If $j<k$ then $\HT(2^k)-\HT(2^j)\ge 2^{-j}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The difference is $2/2^j-2/2^k$ and $2/2^k\le 2/2^{j+1}=1/2^j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Isolation]
If $|\HT(2^j)-\HT(2^k)|<2^{-k}$ then $j=k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Four-decimal row identification]\label{cor:rowid}
If $k\le13$ and $|\HT(2^j)-\HT(2^k)|<10^{-4}$ then $j=k$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$2^{13}=8192\le10^4$, so $10^{-4}\le2^{-k}$ for $k\le13$.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:rowid} is a stronger certificate than Theorem~\ref{thm:four}
in its domain: it uses the minimum spacing of a sparse ladder rather than
denominator size, and identifies rows with denominator $2^{k-1}=4096$, far beyond
the generic bound.

\subsection{The capacity law}

Writing $J_k=(4^k-1)/3$ for the $k$-th Jacobsthal number
($1,5,21,85,341,1365,5461,\dots$),
\[
  \Ipair(2^k)=\frac43\left(1-4^{-k}\right)=\frac{J_k}{4^{k-1}},
\]
so the recorded numerators $5,21,85$ at $k=2,3,4$ are Jacobsthal numbers. The
values are established by evaluation for $1\le k\le4$ and confirmed by exhaustive
enumeration at $k=5,6,7$, where the enumerated values $341/256$, $1365/1024$,
$5461/4096$ agree exactly. The structural reason is a two-branch recursion: in
$\Z/2^k$ the splitting type is a function of the $2$-adic valuation alone, and
$v(x+y)=\min(v(x),v(y))$ except on the diagonal $v(x)=v(y)$, which is precisely
the branching generating $J_{k+1}=4J_k+1$. A proof for all $k$ is the principal
open problem of this work.

\section{The irrational stratum}\label{sec:irr}

\begin{lemma}[Power comparison]\label{lem:pow}
Let $p,q\in\N$, $q>0$. If $2^p<3^q$ then $p/q<\log_2 3$; if $3^q<2^p$ then
$\log_2 3<p/q$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\log_2$ is strictly increasing, $\log_2 2^p=p$ and $\log_2 3^q=q\log_2 3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:irr3}
$\log_2 3\ne m/q$ for all $m,q\in\N$ with $q>0$; hence $\log_2 3$ is irrational.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\log_2 3=m/q$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:pow} neither $2^m<3^q$ nor $3^q<2^m$,
so $2^m=3^q$; but $3\mid 3^q$ and $3\nmid 2^m$. Since $\log_2 3>0$, a rational
value could be written as a quotient of naturals.
\end{proof}

No transcendence theory is used: the obstruction is unique factorisation.

\begin{corollary}
$\Ipair(6)=\log_2 3-1/9$ is irrational, so $\Ipair(6)\ne a/q$ for every integer
$a$ and natural $q$. In this stratum no recorded decimal, of any length, equals
the audited quantity.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Enclosure]\label{thm:encl}
$1.58496<\log_2 3<1.58497$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The convergents $1054/665$ and $485/306$ give, by Lemma~\ref{lem:pow}, the bounds
$1054/665<\log_2 3<485/306$; each reduces to a single integer inequality,
$2^{1054}<3^{665}$ and $3^{306}<2^{485}$, verifiable by exact integer arithmetic.
Finally $1.58496<1054/665$ and $485/306<1.58497$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\left|\Ipair(6)-1.4738\right|<10^{-4}$: the stored record is a valid
four-decimal enclosure, though never an equality.
\end{corollary}

\section{The rationality dichotomy on constructible orders}\label{sec:dich}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:exp}
If $n\ge1$, $b>0$, $a\ge0$ and $b\log_2 n=a$, then $n^b=2^a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:2pow}
If $n\ge1$, $b>0$ and $n^b=2^a$, then $n$ is a power of two.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Any prime $p\mid n$ divides $2^a$, hence $p=2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rationality of binary logarithms]\label{thm:lograt}
For $n\ge1$, $\log_2 n$ is rational if and only if $n$ is a power of two.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $\log_2 n=a/b$ with $b>0$ and $a\ge0$ (as $\log_2 n\ge0$) and apply
Lemmas~\ref{lem:exp} and~\ref{lem:2pow}; conversely $\log_2 2^k=k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Constructible order]
Call $n\ge1$ \emph{constructible} if $\varphi(d)$ is a power of two for every
$d\mid n$. By Gauss--Wantzel these are exactly the $n$ for which the regular
$n$-gon is constructible by straightedge and compass: $n=2^a p_1\cdots p_s$ with
distinct Fermat primes $p_i\in\{3,5,17,257,65537,\dots\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:intsum}
If $n$ is constructible then $\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(d)\log_2\varphi(d)\in\N$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Dichotomy for the type channel]\label{thm:dich}
Let $n\ge1$ be constructible. Then $\HT(n)\in\Q$ if and only if $n$ is a power of
two. In that case $\HT(2^k)=2-2^{1-k}$ is dyadic and certifiable by a
four-decimal record; otherwise $\HT(n)$ is irrational and no record of any
precision can certify it.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemmas~\ref{lem:div} and~\ref{lem:intsum}, $\HT(n)=\log_2 n-c$ with $c\in\Q$;
apply Theorem~\ref{thm:lograt}, and Theorem~\ref{thm:law} for the closed form.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
$\HT(3)=\log_2 3-\tfrac23\approx0.9182958$,
$\HT(5)=\log_2 5-\tfrac85\approx0.7219281$,
$\HT(15)=\log_2 15-\tfrac{34}{15}\approx1.6402239$,
$\HT(17)=\log_2 17-\tfrac{64}{17}\approx0.3227570$: all irrational, all
non-certifiable, while every $\HT(2^k)$ is dyadic and certifiable.
\end{example}

\section{Reproducibility by construction: invariance and its converse}
\label{sec:inv}

Identifying an abstract cyclic Galois group with $\Z/n$ requires choosing a
generator; two choices differ by $x\mapsto u\cdot x$ with $u$ a unit modulo $n$.
Nothing else in the pipeline is arbitrary.

\begin{definition}[Readout]
A \emph{readout} is a map $t:\Z/n\to\N$. Its histogram is the list of occupation
numbers $\#\{x: t(x)=d\}$ over realised values $d$, and $H(t)$ is the entropy of
the resulting distribution.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Relabelling invariance]\label{prop:relabel}
For any readout $t$ and permutation $e$ of $\Z/n$: the histogram of $t\circ e$ is
a permutation of that of $t$; occupation numbers sum to $n$; and
$H(t\circ e)=H(t)$ exactly.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The type is a class function]\label{thm:class}
If $\gcd(u,n)=1$ then $T(u\cdot a)=T(a)$ for every $a$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\gcd(n,ua)=\gcd(n,a)$ for a unit $u$, and $T$ depends only on that $\gcd$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Generator independence of the record]
For every unit $u$ the type histogram, the entropy $\HT(n)$ and the unordered
semiprime type pair are literally unchanged by $x\mapsto u\cdot x$. Any
relabelling of the norm classes merely permutes the rows of the conditional
table, leaving the averaged conditional entropy --- hence $\Ipair(n)$ ---
unchanged.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Orbits are $\gcd$ classes]\label{thm:orbit}
Let $n>0$ and $a,b\in\Z/n$ with $\gcd(n,a)=\gcd(n,b)$. Then $u\cdot a\equiv b
\pmod n$ for some unit $u$ modulo $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $g$ be the common $\gcd$ and write $n=gm$, $a=ga_1$, $b=gb_1$. Cancelling $g$
shows $a_1,b_1$ are units modulo $m$. The unit $w=b_1a_1^{-1}$ of $\Z/m$ lifts to
a unit $u$ of $\Z/n$ along the surjection
$(\Z/n)^\times\twoheadrightarrow(\Z/m)^\times$, and multiplying
$ua_1\equiv b_1\pmod m$ by $g$ gives $ua\equiv b\pmod n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:typgcd}
For $n>0$, $T(x)=T(y)$ if and only if $\gcd(n,x)=\gcd(n,y)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Invariance $\iff$ factorisation]\label{thm:univ}
A readout $t:\Z/n\to\N$ satisfies $t(u\cdot x)=t(x)$ for every unit $u$ and every
$x$ if and only if $t=f\circ T$ for some $f:\N\to\N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:class}. ($\Rightarrow$) If $T(x)=T(y)$ then by
Lemma~\ref{lem:typgcd} and Theorem~\ref{thm:orbit} a unit carries $x$ to $y$, so
$t(x)=t(y)$; define $f$ fibrewise on realised types.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Universality]
The splitting type is invariant and every invariant readout factors through it;
hence every invariant readout has the same histogram and entropy as the induced
function of the type, and $T$ is, up to relabelling of its values, the finest
reproducible observable of a single residue. Anything strictly finer
distinguishes elements within a $\gcd$ class and is an artefact of the coordinate
choice.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}\label{sec:algo}

\paragraph{A. Farey certificate.} Input: recorded $a/q$, re-run $b/r$, tolerance
$\varepsilon$. Test $\varepsilon qr\le1$, then $|ar-bq|<qr\varepsilon$ in exact
integer arithmetic; output CERTIFIED or INCONCLUSIVE. Cost $O(1)$ big-integer
operations. Soundness is Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}; incompleteness is unavoidable by
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}.

\paragraph{B. Channel evaluation by enumeration.} Compute $T(a)$ for all $a$ in
$O(n\log n)$, accumulate the type histogram, then loop over all $(x,y)$
accumulating the joint table of $(K,N)$: $O(n^2)$ time, feasible to
$n\approx2^{13}$. The double loop can be replaced by a convolution over $\gcd$
classes, giving $O(d(n)^2 n)$ with $d(n)$ the number of divisors.

\paragraph{C. Closed-form evaluation.} Return the exact fractions
$\HT(2^k)=(2^k-1)/2^{k-1}$ and $\Ipair(2^k)=J_k/4^{k-1}$ in $O(k)$ bit
operations: an exponential speed-up over enumeration that also yields exact
rationals, which is what the Farey certificate consumes.

\paragraph{D. Stratum classifier.} Factor $n$; if $n$ is a power of two return
RATIONAL-CERTIFIABLE (Theorem~\ref{thm:law}); else if every divisor has
power-of-two totient return IRRATIONAL-NONCERTIFIABLE (Theorem~\ref{thm:dich});
else UNDECIDED. Cost dominated by factorisation and $O(d(n))$ totient
evaluations.

\paragraph{E. Enclosure certificate.} Generate continued-fraction convergents
$p/q$ of $\log_2 3$, accepting $p/q$ as lower (resp.\ upper) bound when
$2^p<3^q$ (resp.\ $3^q<2^p$). The witnesses used here are $2^{1054}<3^{665}$ and
$3^{306}<2^{485}$.

