\section{Discussion}\label{sec:disc}

\subsection{A taxonomy of audit verdicts}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Certified equality.} The recorded quantity is rational with
  denominator below the Farey threshold for the stated precision; digit agreement
  is a proof of bit-for-bit equality, independent of the pipeline.
  \item \textbf{Validated enclosure.} The recorded quantity is a closed-form
  irrational; no record equals it. Digit agreement establishes a correct
  approximation (given an independent enclosure) and determinism, never
  exactness.
  \item \textbf{Structural reproduction.} The recorded quantity is invariant
  under all arbitrary choices in its own definition; it reproduces even under
  re-implementation, because no other value is available.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Design consequences}
Record small-denominator rationals when possible: certifiability is chosen with
the observable. Prefer separation to precision: Corollary~\ref{cor:rowid}
certifies rows with denominators up to $4096$ because the family is sparse, where
the generic bound stops at $70$. In the irrational stratum report closed forms
rather than decimals. Audit invariance, not just determinism: fixed seeds
reproduce one's conventions along with one's results, whereas
Theorem~\ref{thm:univ} characterises exactly which observables are convention
free.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}
Theorem~\ref{thm:dich} assumes constructibility only to make the totient
correction rational; the general statement needs $\Q$-linear independence of
$\{\log p\}$. The capacity law is proved by evaluation for $k\le4$ and confirmed
by enumeration for $k\le7$. The Farey certificate is sound but incomplete.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{From ``the numbers reproduce'' to ``the numbers are theorems''.}
This work replaced the \emph{audit} reading of reproducibility (re-run the
script, compare digits) by a \emph{structural} one: a recorded number is
reproducible when it is either a bounded-denominator rational, in which case a
rounded record provably determines it, or a closed-form irrational, in which case
only the derivation reproduces it --- and the record is invariant under the
arbitrary choices in the pipeline. Four structural patterns emerged:
certifiability is a denominator statement; the dyadic family is a law, not a
table; constructibility controls the stratum; and invariance characterises, not
merely accompanies, the auditable observables.

\paragraph{Direction 1 --- Jacobsthal capacity law.} Prove
$\Ipair(2^k)=\tfrac43(1-4^{-k})$ for all $k$. In $\Z/2^k$ the splitting type is a
function of the $2$-adic valuation alone, and $v(x+y)=\min(v(x),v(y))$ except on
the diagonal, so the conditional type-pair distribution given the norm class
satisfies a two-branch recursion in $k$ --- exactly the recursion producing the
Jacobsthal numbers. The closed form for $\HT(2^k)$ supplies the marginal half of
the computation; only the conditional half is missing.

\paragraph{Direction 2 --- Rationality dichotomy in general.} Drop
constructibility. Since
$\HT(n)=\log_2 n-\frac1n\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(d)\log_2\varphi(d)$ is a
$\Q$-linear combination of logarithms of integers, its rationality is governed by
$\Q$-linear independence of $\{\log p:p\text{ prime}\}$, a consequence of unique
factorisation rather than of transcendence theory.

\paragraph{Direction 3 --- Optimal certificates from separation data.} For a
recorded family with known minimum separation $\delta$, a record at precision
$\varepsilon<\delta/2$ identifies the member uniquely whatever the denominators.
Determining $\delta$ for the pair-capacity family and for mixed-order families
$\{\HT(n):n\le N\}$ would give certificates far beyond denominator bounds.

\paragraph{Direction 4 --- Invariance beyond a single residue.} Universality is
proved for readouts of one residue; the semiprime channel reads out pairs, where
the relevant action is the diagonal unit action on $\Z/n\times\Z/n$.
Characterising its invariants would extend ``reproducible by construction'' from
the marginal record to the full conditional table.

\section{Conclusion}

A reproducibility audit that compares decimals is doing arithmetic, and the
arithmetic has a precise reach. Distinct rationals of denominator at most $70$
cannot agree to four decimals, so within that stratum a matching record is a
theorem of exact equality; past denominator $100$ the guarantee fails, as
$1/100$ and $1/101$ witness. For the cyclic splitting-type channel this
partitions the record: the two-power entropies $\HT(2^k)=2-2^{1-k}$ and
capacities $\Ipair(2^k)=\tfrac43(1-4^{-k})$ are dyadic and certifiable --- indeed
the four audited entropy rows are the first four instances of a law with a
two-bit ceiling and a $2^{-k}$ row gap --- whereas $\Ipair(6)=\log_2 3-1/9$ and,
more generally, $\HT(n)$ for every constructible non-two-power $n$, are
irrational and beyond the reach of any decimal record. There the audit certifies
an enclosure, not a value. Finally the whole record is invariant under the single
arbitrary choice the pipeline makes --- the generator of the cyclic group --- and
invariance characterises exactly the observables that factor through the
splitting type. The strongest form of reproducibility available is therefore not
``the same code gives the same digits'' but ``no correct implementation could
have produced anything else''.

